games content: E_CONTENT_FORMAT, so a duplicate key cannot make an avatar look plain

The games gate parsed glTF JSON with json.loads, which keeps the LAST of
two members with the same name. A GLB whose JSON named `extensions`
twice, an avatar that prohibits redistribution first and `{}` second,
read as plain. 516ba38 judges plain glTF only in character parts, so in
an animations part that file published with no licence verdict at all.
minice-01 found the same shape in the content catalogue
(animset-glb-duplicate-key) after this branch's review, and pointed at
it here. A JSON chunk declared past EOF and an https image uri passed as
well.

Every GLB and .gltf, in any part and of any class, is now held to the
content catalogue's step 10 BEFORE it is classified, and a failure is
E_CONTENT_FORMAT:
- the GLB is whole (its header length is the file size; its JSON chunk
  ends inside the file);
- its JSON parses with no member named twice;
- a GLB's images[]/buffers[] uris are data: only;
- a .gltf's uris are data: or a plain relative path to a file the
  release holds, so nothing Astra loads makes it fetch a URL that tells a
  third party who is looking.

New goldens: vrm-duplicate-key, glb-duplicate-extensions,
vrm-chunk-past-eof, vrm-header-length-wrong, glb-remote-uri, glb-data-uri
(accepted as unknown), and the release goldens animation-duplicate-key,
gltf-uri-escape and gltf-relative-uri (accepted). Mutations: parsing
leniently again fails 3 goldens; letting any uri through fails 2.

